WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.265 --> 00:00:15.047
Miss Martha Meacham kept the little bakery on the corner (the one where you go up three steps,

2
00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:17.308
and the bell tinkles when you open the door).

3
00:00:18.588 --> 00:00:24.591
Miss Martha was forty, her bankbook showed a credit of two thousand dollars, and she possessed two false

4
00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:26.332
teeth and a sympathetic heart.

5
00:00:27.592 --> 00:00:31.816
Many people have married whose chances to do so were much inferior to Miss Martha's.

6
00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:37.500
Two or three times a week, a customer came in whom she began to take an interest.

7
00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:44.045
He was a middle-aged man, wearing spectacles and a brown beard trimmed to a careful point.

8
00:00:45.106 --> 00:00:47.528
He spoke English with a strong German accent.

9
00:00:48.729 --> 00:00:53.033
His clothes were worn and darned in places, and wrinkled and baggy in others.

10
00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.716
But he looked neat and had very good manners.

11
00:00:58.156 --> 00:01:00.497
He always bought two loaves of stale bread.

12
00:01:01.578 --> 00:01:03.179
Fresh bread was five cents a loaf.

13
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.961
Stale ones were two for five.

14
00:01:06.781 --> 00:01:09.082
Never did he call for anything but stale bread.

15
00:01:10.523 --> 00:01:13.485
Once, Miss Martha saw a red and brown stain on his fingers.

16
00:01:14.566 --> 00:01:17.387
She was sure then that he was an artist and very poor.

17
00:01:18.428 --> 00:01:22.971
No doubt he lived in a garret, where he painted pictures and ate stale bread and thought of

18
00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:25.192
the good things to eat in Miss Martha's bakery.

19
00:01:26.416 --> 00:01:31.380
Often when Miss Martha sat down to her chops and light rolls and jam and tea, she would

20
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:37.624
sigh and wish that the gentle-mannered artist might share her tasty meal instead of eating his dry crust

21
00:01:37.684 --> 00:01:38.745
in that drafty attic.

22
00:01:39.925 --> 00:01:44.128
Miss Martha's heart, as you have been told, was a sympathetic one.

23
00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:49.972
In order to test her theory as to his occupation, she brought from her room one day a

24
00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:54.836
painting that she had bought at a sale, and set it against the shelves behind the bread counter.

25
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.321
It was a Venetian scene.

26
00:01:58.281 --> 00:02:04.225
A splendid marble palazzio (so it said on the picture) stood in the foreground─or rather forewater.

27
00:02:05.646 --> 00:02:12.109
For the rest, there were gondolas (with the lady trailing her hand in the water), clouds, sky, and

28
00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:14.251
beautiful play of light and shadow in plenty.

29
00:02:15.431 --> 00:02:17.272
No artist could fail to notice it.

30
00:02:18.153 --> 00:02:20.234
Two days afterward, the customer came in.

31
00:02:20.254 --> 00:02:23.416
"Two loaves of stale bread, if you please.

32
00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:29.608
You have here a fine picture, madame," he said while she was wrapping up the bread.

33
00:02:29.628 --> 00:02:34.372
"Yes?" says Miss Martha, reveling in her own cunning.

34
00:02:34.392 --> 00:02:43.079
"I do so admire art and"—no, it would not do to say "artists" so early—"and paintings," she substituted.

35
00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:45.621
"You think it is a good picture?"

36
00:02:45.641 --> 00:02:50.765
"The balance," said the customer, "is not in good drawing.

37
00:02:51.866 --> 00:02:53.768
The perspective of it is not true.

38
00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:55.028
Good morning, madame."

39
00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:57.910
He took his bread, bowed, and hurried out.

40
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.591
Yes, he must be an artist.

41
00:03:01.071 --> 00:03:03.192
Miss Martha took the picture back to her room.

42
00:03:04.353 --> 00:03:07.694
How gently and kindly his eyes shone behind his spectacles!

43
00:03:08.375 --> 00:03:09.915
What a broad brow he had!

44
00:03:11.056 --> 00:03:15.058
To be able to judge perspective at a glance─and to live on stale bread!

45
00:03:16.038 --> 00:03:19.380
But genius often has to struggle before it is recognized.

46
00:03:20.392 --> 00:03:25.054
What a thing it would be for art and perspective if genius were backed by two thousand dollars

47
00:03:25.094 --> 00:03:31.557
in the bank, a bakery, and a sympathetic heart to─But these were daydreams, Miss Martha.

48
00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:36.459
Often now when he came he would chat for a while across the showcase.

49
00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:40.101
He seemed to crave Miss Martha's cheerful words.

50
00:03:41.301 --> 00:03:49.072
He kept on buying stale bread─never a cake, never a pie, never one of her delicious Sally Lunns.

51
00:03:49.092 --> 00:03:51.893
She thought he began to look thinner and discouraged.

52
00:03:52.793 --> 00:03:58.075
Her heart ached to add something good to eat to his meager purchase, but her courage failed at

53
00:03:58.115 --> 00:03:58.575
the act.

54
00:03:59.655 --> 00:04:01.055
She did not dare affront him.

55
00:04:01.776 --> 00:04:03.376
She knew the pride of artists.

56
00:04:04.896 --> 00:04:08.837
Miss Martha took to wearing her blue-dotted silk blouse behind the counter.

57
00:04:09.898 --> 00:04:14.359
In the back room, she cooked a mysterious compound of quince seeds and borax.

58
00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:17.780
Ever so many people use it for the complexion.

59
00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:24.226
One day, the customer came in as usual, laid his nickel on the showcase, and called for his

60
00:04:24.266 --> 00:04:25.067
stale loaves.

61
00:04:26.207 --> 00:04:31.289
While Miss Martha was reaching for them, there was a great tooting and clanging, and a fire engine

62
00:04:31.329 --> 00:04:32.450
came lumbering past.

63
00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.691
The customer hurried to the door to look, as anyone will.

64
00:04:37.732 --> 00:04:40.733
Suddenly inspired, Miss Martha seized the opportunity.

65
00:04:41.853 --> 00:04:46.875
On the bottom shelf behind the counter was a pound of fresh butter that the dairyman had left

66
00:04:46.956 --> 00:04:47.796
ten minutes before.

67
00:04:48.748 --> 00:04:53.990
With a bread knife, Miss Martha made a deep slash in each of the stale loaves, inserted a

68
00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:57.251
generous quantity of butter, and pressed the loaves tight again.

69
00:04:58.351 --> 00:05:02.132
When the customer turned once more, she was tying the paper around them.

70
00:05:03.672 --> 00:05:10.414
When he had gone, after an unusually pleasant little chat, Miss Martha smiled to herself, but not without

71
00:05:10.454 --> 00:05:12.095
a slight fluttering of the heart.

72
00:05:13.055 --> 00:05:14.155
Had she been too bold?

73
00:05:14.696 --> 00:05:15.616
Would he take offense?

74
00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.132
But surely not.

75
00:05:17.512 --> 00:05:19.153
There was no language of edibles.

76
00:05:20.213 --> 00:05:22.834
Butter was no emblem of unmaidenly forwardness.

77
00:05:23.714 --> 00:05:26.715
For a long time that day, her mind dwelt on the subject.

78
00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:30.796
She imagined the scene when he should discover her little deception.

79
00:05:32.136 --> 00:05:34.357
He would lay down his brushes and palette.

80
00:05:35.377 --> 00:05:40.759
There would stand his easel with the picture he was painting in which the perspective was beyond criticism.

81
00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:44.640
He would prepare for his luncheon of dry bread and water.

82
00:05:45.632 --> 00:05:48.154
He would slice into a loaf─ah!

83
00:05:48.774 --> 00:05:49.975
Miss Martha blushed.

84
00:05:51.116 --> 00:05:53.758
Would he think of the hand that placed it there as he ate?

85
00:05:54.238 --> 00:05:57.601
Would he─ The front door bell jangled viciously.

86
00:05:58.741 --> 00:06:01.423
Somebody was coming in, making a great deal of noise.

87
00:06:02.604 --> 00:06:04.105
Miss Martha hurried to the front.

88
00:06:04.826 --> 00:06:05.666
Two men were there.

89
00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:11.170
One was a young man smoking a pipe─a man she had never seen before.

90
00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.232
The other was her artist.

91
00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:19.463
His face was very red, his hat was on the back of his head, his hair was wildly

92
00:06:19.523 --> 00:06:24.626
rumpled. He clenched his two fists and shook them ferociously at Miss Martha.

93
00:06:25.647 --> 00:06:26.907
"You stupid woman!"

94
00:06:27.548 --> 00:06:31.170
he shouted with extreme loudness; and then "Tausendonfer!"

95
00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:33.311
or something like it in German.

96
00:06:34.271 --> 00:06:36.052
The young man tried to draw him away.

97
00:06:36.072 --> 00:06:42.036
"I will not go," he said angrily, "before I have told her."

98
00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.241
He made a bass drum of Miss Martha's counter.

99
00:06:46.382 --> 00:06:51.204
"You have ruined me," he cried, his blue eyes blazing behind his spectacles.

100
00:06:51.224 --> 00:06:53.485
"I will tell you.

101
00:06:53.885 --> 00:06:55.746
You are a meddlesome old cat!"

102
00:06:56.926 --> 00:07:02.389
Miss Martha leaned weakly against the shelves and laid one hand on her blue-dotted silk blouse.

103
00:07:03.329 --> 00:07:05.310
The young man took the other by the collar.

104
00:07:06.471 --> 00:07:09.192
"Come on," he said, "you've said enough."

105
00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.781
He dragged the angry one out the door to the sidewalk, and then came back.

106
00:07:13.801 --> 00:07:19.242
"Guess you ought to be told, ma'am," he said, "what the row is about.

107
00:07:20.103 --> 00:07:21.023
That's Blumberger.

108
00:07:21.463 --> 00:07:23.024
He's an architectural draftsman.

109
00:07:23.444 --> 00:07:24.844
I work in the same office with him.

110
00:07:24.864 --> 00:07:29.745
"He's been working hard for three months drawing a plan for a new city hall.

111
00:07:30.726 --> 00:07:32.186
It was a prize competition.

112
00:07:32.906 --> 00:07:34.687
He finished inking the lines yesterday.

113
00:07:35.767 --> 00:07:39.188
You know, a draftsman always makes his drawing in pencil first.

114
00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.241
When it's done, he rubs out the pencil lines with handfuls of stale bread crumbs.

115
00:07:45.341 --> 00:07:46.922
That's better than India rubber.

116
00:07:46.942 --> 00:07:49.923
"Blumberger's been buying the bread here.

117
00:07:51.063 --> 00:08:00.046
Well, today─well, you know, ma'am, that butter isn't─well, Blumberger's plan isn't good for anything now except to wrap

118
00:08:00.106 --> 00:08:00.826
up sandwiches."

119
00:08:02.286 --> 00:08:04.067
Miss Martha went into the back room.

120
00:08:05.307 --> 00:08:10.716
She took off the blue-dotted silk blouse and put on the old brown wool shirt she used to

121
00:08:10.736 --> 00:08:16.984
wear. Then she poured the quince seed and borax mixture out of the window into the ash can.
